#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166669 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "b301b73e-1d26-56b7-bb21-33d6acae8d5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8557" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(6, 0.7),$ compute $P(X = 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(6, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{6}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2})(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{6-2} \\\\  & =15(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.0595\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166670 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "449e6e8f-0e9b-5208-8945-1ce3ea3d3dbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8558" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ compute $P(0 <     X < 3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} (0.3)^x (0.7)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.3)(0.7)^{4}+10(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.6689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166671 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "ae2b59a4-d3d6-53ea-aca2-93cbb2da7478" :question/math-academy-id "e-8559" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ compute $P(X \\geq 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{7}{x} (0.2)^x (0.8)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X \\geq 2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2, 3, 4, \\ldots, 7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X < 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0, 1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X < 2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{7-1} \\\\  & =(0.8)^{7}+7(0.2)(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.5767\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.5767 \\\\  & =0.4233\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166672 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "aeaecf43-9fce-552c-a3f8-69a2f09f2d9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8564" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ compute $P(X > 8).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{10}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{10-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X\\in \\{9,10\\}) \\\\  & =f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10} \\\\  & =10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.1493\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d1eb21b-efcf-521b-9e07-dbb7ef9f6da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{128}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426379} {:answer/id #uuid "74d1dea8-3001-57c1-bb93-a5813432ff19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426386} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9519b0-0a62-557f-bec1-cdd70ddafa24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426396} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1b94f5-a8bc-5ef0-94bb-1d5c14e5fa17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426403} {:answer/id #uuid "f9908702-dfd9-51a4-86da-cae4fb4e34d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9908702-dfd9-51a4-86da-cae4fb4e34d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426409} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c96047-8dc3-5b20-819f-e33bb3985261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0fe361b-76eb-5e77-a52f-d24556387da9" :question/math-academy-id "q-129854" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ compute $P(X=4).$" :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-4} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b8803bc-c41e-54ae-b79d-f7704c1672b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5255" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426382} {:answer/id #uuid "929040eb-c107-5a59-9d55-ce705755f16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426389} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3119c4-662c-5c4b-b111-9a52bb6e8cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426395} {:answer/id #uuid "e01569c6-f716-5054-817e-a939df208775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426406} {:answer/id #uuid "f7141707-0275-54eb-be63-9da647e13e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab3119c4-662c-5c4b-b111-9a52bb6e8cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1875" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426395} :answer-field/id #uuid "13fb84a1-422c-5744-9ac6-88da6477ca55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a63141b0-e3e5-58fd-a59c-49398986fa85" :question/math-academy-id "q-129970" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(5,0.5),$ compute $P(X>3).$" :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =P(X\\in \\{4,5\\}) \\\\  & =f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.5)^{4}(0.5)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.5)^{5}(0.5)^{5-5} \\\\  & =5(0.5)^{4}(0.5)^{1}+1(0.5)^{5}(0.5)^{0} \\\\  & =0.1875.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f09ccff-b719-5706-b633-2ad30710e20a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{83}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263286} {:answer/id #uuid "6c14e663-94cd-5254-8817-fe604dbc101d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263932} {:answer/id #uuid "9533e4e9-83b9-5758-b941-dc67892e3add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{160}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264277} {:answer/id #uuid "9c77157c-53f0-5174-a00c-754cd60c5e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{65}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264339} {:answer/id #uuid "f6da63d1-d112-5df8-a4c2-6b108d477a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9533e4e9-83b9-5758-b941-dc67892e3add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{160}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264277} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e5cc49-beb6-5262-a154-ccd9ccda51e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0316062-8105-57c8-93fe-257f0fc7faf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129973" :question/problem "Given $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ compute $P(1\\le X\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-2} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}+10{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{160}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01db9526-8dcd-5f42-a095-1323e6d73cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0988" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426371} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad0de23-e75c-53db-bbd9-2e2c3c9e8852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9421" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426381} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f27407-6708-579c-8dd4-c118aced1d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9933" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426398} {:answer/id #uuid "ccca07ff-3b57-5840-a44a-ae67c0a52c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426402} {:answer/id #uuid "de2fac65-ca56-5d6d-bd14-98f17e6aaf60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01db9526-8dcd-5f42-a095-1323e6d73cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0988" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426371} :answer-field/id #uuid "f314efa0-776a-52a7-9713-20e149f2b5b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a8ce807-1d45-5332-844a-554a40b8b81d" :question/math-academy-id "q-129974" :question/problem "Given $X∼B(6,0.2),$ compute $P(3\\le X\\le 5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{6-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{6-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5} \\\\  & =20(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{3}+15(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{2}+6(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{1} \\\\  & ≈0.0988\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a99b9af-62d2-5730-ab23-bea1a25e4377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426374} {:answer/id #uuid "57474101-594b-51dc-83d8-9ccefe0cde93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426376} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc53b4e-9a2a-5cc8-9496-1f0f937e2c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426377} {:answer/id #uuid "721df6eb-4f71-53ae-8f64-290c947bd23e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9963" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426385} {:answer/id #uuid "9e74eca7-137b-5101-aaa1-e37343e6009c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0266" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57474101-594b-51dc-83d8-9ccefe0cde93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426376} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d002d9-47dc-5072-8abc-c337fb7824ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d3c970b3-ed82-5453-989f-95b7ad9d86a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-129985" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(11,0.4),$ compute $P(X>1).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(11,0.4),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{11-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X>1)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2,3,…,11.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\le 1)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{11-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{11-1} \\\\  & =1(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{11}+11(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{10} \\\\  & ≈0.0302\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & =1-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0302 \\\\  & =0.9698\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dac87971-ab65-5b84-9c12-22d190895c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.141" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dac87971-ab65-5b84-9c12-22d190895c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.141" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426404} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c61947c-0d22-57ad-bebb-5efd43494a53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4482a30-821e-5dc4-b28b-016916e46721" :question/math-academy-id "q-275955" :question/problem "If $X∼B(3,\\frac{3}{4}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X=1)≈${{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,\\frac{3}{4}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-1} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.141\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n rounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eaa6af1-ae05-584a-ab0f-cea039ff6116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.222" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eaa6af1-ae05-584a-ab0f-cea039ff6116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.222" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426383} :answer-field/id #uuid "21767cab-3483-528c-8083-c4d9660b90a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8dd92dbf-67ea-5e4b-a006-5fb0637bcddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-279595" :question/problem "If $X∼B(3,\\frac{2}{3}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X=1)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3-1} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cba73ae8-c786-52e4-ab99-0ba9d5c16623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.303" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cba73ae8-c786-52e4-ab99-0ba9d5c16623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.303" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426401} :answer-field/id #uuid "599fa3b3-c002-57ad-9832-d0236fedbe8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2704acc-87d3-5c87-a9ec-e5a36c5fdd82" :question/math-academy-id "q-279613" :question/problem "If $X∼B(6,0.3),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X=1)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{6-1} \\\\  & =6(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.303\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee28f110-16f2-58b4-83fc-87f35c23ca7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.164" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee28f110-16f2-58b4-83fc-87f35c23ca7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.164" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426407} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1900926-7d6e-58d3-9d59-0ea4f28806ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7cbb7fd-23f6-5974-bc2c-e1910754e60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-279621" :question/problem "If $X∼B(7,0.5),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X=5)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){(0.5)}^{x}{(0.5)}^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5}){(0.5)}^{5}{(0.5)}^{7-5} \\\\  & =21{(0.5)}^{5}{(0.5)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.164\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aea14743-6088-59c8-8f9a-a58b76360d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea14743-6088-59c8-8f9a-a58b76360d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426397} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c6944a8-5962-57d4-9737-fea05e427ebd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ca97e6a-5efa-581b-b908-666fe530263f" :question/math-academy-id "q-279685" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,0.6),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\ge 4)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =P(X\\in \\{4,5\\}) \\\\  & =f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.6)^{4}(0.4)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.6)^{5}(0.4)^{5-5} \\\\  & =5(0.6)^{4}(0.4)^{1}+1(0.6)^{5}(0.4)^{0} \\\\  & =0.337\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2868a9b8-174d-5afc-bb08-ca49c79aaf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.313" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2868a9b8-174d-5afc-bb08-ca49c79aaf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.313" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426372} :answer-field/id #uuid "310bee73-be4e-5ea2-a6ca-7ecc03fc0335" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2083b042-5bf1-5223-9478-ae910fe13957" :question/math-academy-id "q-279687" :question/problem "If $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X>2)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-4} \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}+1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.313\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "847105dc-ec17-5c51-b3bd-459142f718fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "847105dc-ec17-5c51-b3bd-459142f718fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426388} :answer-field/id #uuid "b54d936c-e9c0-55ec-8600-34e7d9ffd887" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75d3c9e3-0aae-5212-8d84-2a70ca742fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-279695" :question/problem "If $X∼B(4,0.3),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<2)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(0.3)}^{x}{(0.7)}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(0.3)}^{0}{(0.7)}^{4-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(0.3)}^{1}{(0.7)}^{4-1} \\\\  & =1{(0.3)}^{0}{(0.7)}^{4}+4{(0.3)}^{1}{(0.7)}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.652\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bc4b166-784b-5fe5-b467-90eddb4a59b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bc4b166-784b-5fe5-b467-90eddb4a59b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426387} :answer-field/id #uuid "d650aabb-97f2-5442-bad3-a63b13ee3005" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0c29cf6-86b2-58fd-b05e-41adbe40c41c" :question/math-academy-id "q-279701" :question/problem "If $X∼B(6,\\frac{1}{2}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(2\\le X\\le 3)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6-2}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6-3} \\\\  & =15{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}+20{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.547\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9982983a-8e0f-5f7c-a1ee-5e88b1fed507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9982983a-8e0f-5f7c-a1ee-5e88b1fed507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293538} :answer-field/id #uuid "25a558c6-1763-51bc-b357-3b5fc6761fa9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d9c0c2e-8f50-55f5-816f-cbabaaec51c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-279767" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,0.1),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places** $P(0<X\\le 2)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.1),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.1)^{x}(0.9)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{4}+10(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.401\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c37f2bf-cb72-57fb-bcc3-45d5d5fd4543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.991" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c37f2bf-cb72-57fb-bcc3-45d5d5fd4543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.991" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426378} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b1da153-5ec5-5d24-8004-8ddaca1d0c60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5103b67-e208-5c70-9c50-ad3c3ab4c26a" :question/math-academy-id "q-281206" :question/problem "If $X∼B(11,0.65),$  then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 10)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(11,0.65),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{x}){(0.65)}^{x}{(0.35)}^{11-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 10)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,3,…,10.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>10)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=11,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>10),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>10) & =P(X\\in \\{11\\}) \\\\  & =f(11) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{11}){(0.65)}^{11}{(0.35)}^{11-11} \\\\  & =1{(0.65)}^{11}{(0.35)}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.009\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10) & =1-P(X>10) \\\\  & ≈1-0.009 \\\\  & =0.991\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n rounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e468efe-91f0-5d37-a803-a2d31861e386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358167} {:answer/id #uuid "46d8c9a0-c916-5167-aa6f-a527b03eb820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.227" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358361} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4ae096-6e88-52af-bc5a-f83a82896363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358582} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1f14c4-f41c-57b7-a976-80e778ff8a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.273" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358719} {:answer/id #uuid "a99df817-1a95-57b8-82ff-41cb8e115df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e468efe-91f0-5d37-a803-a2d31861e386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358167} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab4151e7-48c9-5220-81f5-230568253159" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32727390-885c-5602-b623-b9ec09406443" :question/math-academy-id "q-97503" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(3,0.3),$ compute $P(X\\ge 2).$" :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3-2}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3})(0.3)^{3}(0.7)^{3-3} \\\\  & =3(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+1(0.3)^{3}(0.7)^{0} \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f7f8f8-6d98-56d1-aa53-20c3e1aa7ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426375} {:answer/id #uuid "66d03ab9-8b56-5757-bbdd-542971a7a77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426380} {:answer/id #uuid "940b1136-40b6-57c2-a8ad-7f290f2f65fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426391} {:answer/id #uuid "a45f4c80-4d46-5c85-b6a4-2b5569f92d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426394} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b6f995-6912-5671-a750-6913fe3d38d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9b6f995-6912-5671-a750-6913fe3d38d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426400} :answer-field/id #uuid "c380aeb9-568e-5bb5-990d-8d181e2da343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f428e76f-4165-56e2-a17c-fa7e27edc2d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97517" :question/problem "Given $X∼B(7,0.3),$ compute $P(6\\le X\\le 7).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6\\le X\\le 7) & =P(X\\in \\{6,7\\}) \\\\  & =f(6)+f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.3)^{6}(0.7)^{7-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.3)^{7}(0.7)^{7-7} \\\\  & =7(0.3)^{6}(0.7)+1(0.3)^{7}(0.7)^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.0038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ce615c1-c056-50fa-a609-0af78e4d7278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361389} {:answer/id #uuid "90bb771b-acb1-5d7b-82d6-e6cb2a2f76c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9078" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361477} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2222aa-6db4-5123-82d5-43e005e9d05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361607} {:answer/id #uuid "eddad30f-5a3e-5f84-ac5e-0779efe69930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9922" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361894} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f90992-a3f4-5ce7-bb9a-05f52ebcdf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eddad30f-5a3e-5f84-ac5e-0779efe69930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9922" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361894} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd6feca6-76ee-5295-b8ca-70efe4a6b0da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd0fa54f-0d49-5cf3-b205-811b4116ac34" :question/math-academy-id "q-97521" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(7,0.5),$ compute $P(X\\le 6).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(X\\in \\{7\\}) \\\\  & =f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{7-7} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.0078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0078 \\\\  & =0.9922\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0331a8e-bc99-561c-93f8-5cb1d0a3f795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166275} {:answer/id #uuid "158ac3a0-c32d-558a-9328-ddd4a9dce690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9619" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166276} {:answer/id #uuid "18c03e0c-9817-5e5d-807c-623abce8cb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166277} {:answer/id #uuid "7095c419-1fbb-5926-98a3-f19fe88d54b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166278} {:answer/id #uuid "7b211696-1127-5bc4-af0c-4be54f99971b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9870" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c03e0c-9817-5e5d-807c-623abce8cb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166277} :answer-field/id #uuid "80c66991-1e2f-5011-a6ac-aa3f758437f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6c0bc50-a84e-5a1e-b63c-9cb2a3ea9f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-97527" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(5,0.6),$ compute $P(X>1).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X>1)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2,3,4,5.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\le 1)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.6)^{0}(0.4)^{5-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.6)^{1}(0.4)^{5-1} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{5}+5(0.6)(0.4)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.0870\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & =1-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0870 \\\\  & =0.9130\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c29ad5a-6441-5af9-ae31-6bd58be51e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4727" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426373} {:answer/id #uuid "6f549d46-1ef7-5a27-b39c-72a2ef520a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4482" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426384} {:answer/id #uuid "93af3f80-2591-55c1-9a84-cef9fe7203b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3518" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426390} {:answer/id #uuid "a30a1569-e802-512e-981f-bc003b02360a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3965" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426393} {:answer/id #uuid "b9587b8d-cd73-5a11-8bd8-8412b245cac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4553" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f549d46-1ef7-5a27-b39c-72a2ef520a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4482" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426384} :answer-field/id #uuid "b665fa20-75cd-5f8d-92da-c59c5b40aabc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b719cf3-bd28-5a38-b0fa-b59090fe757c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97529" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(8,0.3),$ compute $P(X>2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(8,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{8-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X>2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=3,4,…,8.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\le 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{0})(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{8-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{8-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{8-2} \\\\  & =(0.7)^{8}+8(0.3)(0.7)^{7}+28(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.5518\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.5518 \\\\  & =0.4482\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}]}
